body:

## Cold western remake... need to watch the original...

**Genre / Runtime:** Western / 122 min

<figure class="figure">
  <img src="./310_to_yuma_poster.jpg" alt="310_to_yuma_poster">
</figure>

I later learned this film is a remake of a 1957 western, which made me want to track down the original. For the first time in a long while, I watched a movie from start to finish... without pausing... without rewinding. This movie took me back home to family nights with everyone gathered around the heater... dinner... not-so-smart TV...
The film made me appreciate good storytelling again, a movie from an era when films were written for an audience that was not distracted. My father would have enjoyed this one.

The story follows Dan Evans (Christian Bale), a Civil War veteran trying to keep a roof over his family's heads in 1880s Arizona. That gets harder after his ranch is wrecked by crooks in suits collecting a debt he owes to them.

To earn some quick cash, Evans agrees to help transport the captured outlaw Ben Wade (Russell Crowe) to the train that will take him to trial. But Wade and his gang have other plans... cue the evil music.

The film rests on two strong performances from Bale and Crowe. Their characters start out as opposite types of manhood, but by the end they are closer than they first appear.

Bale plays Evans as the film's clear hero, a man the audience is meant to root for. Losing his ranch and risking his life for his family make his stakes personal and real. There's also a quiet sense of powerlessness in the character, which stands out next to Wade's overt confidence.

Crowe, by contrast, plays Wade with real charm and menace, the kind of man other people cannot help but obey. It is unsettling but compelling to watch him deal with people who cross him, especially anyone who insults his late mother.

> "Even bad men love their mummies..." -- Ben Wade

Lines like that could have turned Wade into some cartoon villain, but the performance never lets that happen.

What kept me watching was the tension between the two, not just the build-up to a gunfight, but an actual battle of wills. Wade's quiet manipulation of Evans during the journey feels genuinely persuasive, which makes his change of heart late in the film believable rather than a cheap twist. What was funny was the town's cowardice at the train station... all talk earlier, but nobody actually shows up to help, drives home what the film is really about: the difference between reputation and character.

<span class="margin-note">"Go on. Look down there... They’re animals. All of them... I wouldn’t last five minutes leading an outfit like that if I wasn’t as rotten as Hell." -- Ben Wade</span>

I also liked Charlie Prince, played by Ben Foster, Wade's second-in-command. He gives us a glimpse of how brutal the gang really is under Wade, and how far that loyalty runs. Charlie's presence sets up the tone for the final act.

That final act is where the film loses me a little, a sharp climax... it turns into a full shootout that undercuts the slower, character-driven tension built over the entire story. The last 15 minutes feel like a different, more conventional movie.

Still, *3:10 to Yuma* is a strong mix of good storytelling, exciting action, and performances that carry the whole thing. Thank you, James Mangold... I enjoyed your movie.

Recommended!